= What Marketing Actually Is

A factory can build a perfect product and still watch it gather dust — because
business begins where the *customer's need* begins, not where the production
line ends. Marketing is the discipline of beginning there: understanding what
people need, shaping an offer around it, carrying it to them, telling them
about it, and servicing them after — so that satisfaction and profit arrive
together, and keep arriving.

- *Definition:* marketing is a *social process* by which individuals and
  groups obtain what they need and want through creating, offering and
  exchanging products of value.
- *Its span:* it starts *before production* (what to make, for whom) and
  continues *after the sale* (service, feedback, repeat trust) — selling is
  only one step inside it.
- *Its promise:* profit through *satisfaction* — the customer returns, and
  brings friends.

== The functions of marketing

- *Gathering market information:* studying needs, tastes and rivals before
  decisions are made.
- *Marketing planning:* translating findings into targets, budgets and
  programmes.
- *Product designing and development:* shaping the offer the need demands —
  features, quality, style.
- *Standardisation and grading:* ensuring uniform quality and sorted grades —
  buyers can trust what they get.
- *Packaging and labelling:* protecting the product and speaking for it on
  the shelf.
- *Branding:* giving the product a name and identity buyers can remember and
  repeat.
- *Customer support service:* handling complaints, repairs and queries — the
  after-sale half of the promise.
- *Pricing:* setting the money value that balances cost, demand and rivalry.
- *Promotion:* telling, persuading and reminding — advertising, selling,
  incentives.
- *Distribution:* carrying the product to where the customer can reach it.
- *Transportation and warehousing:* moving goods and storing them till
  needed — time and place utilities created.

== The role of marketing

- *To the firm:* marketing earns the revenue that funds everything else —
  and builds the brand that lets the firm charge fairly.
- *To the consumer:* needs are understood, choices widen, quality improves,
  information flows — the customer gains power.
- *To society:* employment grows across channels, standards of living rise,
  and national income expands as production finds its buyers.

== The marketing philosophies — five eras of thinking

- *Production concept:* make it cheap and available — buyers want affordable
  products (early industrial era).
- *Product concept:* make it better — quality and features will sell
  themselves (the improvement trap: ignoring what buyers actually want).
- *Selling concept:* sell what we make — push through aggressive promotion
  (goods nobody asked for get forced on markets).
- *Marketing concept:* find what the customer wants and make it —
  satisfaction first, profit as the reward (the modern base).
- *Societal marketing concept:* satisfy customers *and* society —
  environment, ethics and welfare join the equation (today's standard).

