- *The memory line:* *selling eyes the product; marketing eyes the customer*
  — and marketing *includes* selling as one of its many functions.

= The Marketing Mix — Tuning the Four Ps

#figure(image("assets/bs_marketing_mix.png", width: 100%), caption: [Figure 1 — The marketing mix: the four Ps with their questions, their sub-elements and the factor lists that tune each one.])

- *The concept:* the marketing mix is the set of *four controllable tools* —
  product, price, place, promotion — that the firm blends to answer the
  needs of its target market.
- *The blend rule:* the four must *fit together* — a premium product with
  discount-shelf distribution sends mixed signals and fails both ways.

= Product — Nature, Classification, Brand, Label, Package

- *Nature:* a product is *anything of value offered to a market* — goods,
  services, or a bundle of both with brand and support attached.
- *Consumer goods — classified by buying effort:*
  - *Convenience goods:* bought often, with minimum effort — bread, soap,
    newspapers; widely spread outlets, low unit price.
  - *Shopping goods:* compared on quality, price and style — clothes, shoes,
    furniture; fewer outlets, selective stocking.
  - *Specialty goods:* buyers make *special effort* to obtain them — luxury
    watches, designer wear; one or few exclusive outlets.
- *Industrial goods:* materials and parts, capital items (plant, machinery),
  and supplies and services — bought for producing other products.

== Branding, labelling, packaging

- *Brand:* the *name, term, sign or symbol* that identifies one seller's
  product and sets it apart — brand name is speakable, brand mark is the
  drawn part, trademark is the legal protection.
- *Advantages:* easy identification, repeat purchase, differential pricing,
  a base for new products.
- *Label:* the *written tag* on the product — identity, contents, weight,
  usage, warnings; labels inform and legally protect.
- *Packaging:* the *wrapper or container* — protection in transit, easy
  handling, self-promotion on the shelf; modern packaging is called the
  *silent salesman*.

= Price — The Only Revenue-Earning P

- *Meaning:* the money value customers pay for the offer — list price,
  discounts, credit terms all included.
- *The sensitivity:* price is the fastest lever and the most dangerous —
  set it above value and buyers flee; below cost and the firm bleeds.

== Factors influencing pricing

- *Cost of the product:* total cost — fixed plus variable — sets the floor
  below which sustained pricing is impossible.
- *Demand and utility:* what buyers can pay and how much they value the
  product — the utility the product creates sets the ceiling.
- *Competition:* rivals' prices bracket the choice — price above a stronger
  rival needs a visible reason.
- *Government regulation:* price ceilings and controls on essentials bind
  the setter's hands.
- *Pricing objectives:* profit max, market share, survival, or premium image —
  the goal chooses the price path.
- *Marketing method used:* distribution and promotion style affects the
  price — premium channels carry premium prices.

= Physical Distribution and Channels of Distribution

Making the product is half the battle; *placing it within the customer's
reach* is the other half — and it is called physical distribution.

- *Meaning:* physical distribution covers all activities that make the
  product *available at the right place and time* — from factory to doorstep.
- *The four components:*
  - *Transportation:* carries goods between points — road, rail, air, water,
    pipeline; creates *place utility*.
  - *Warehousing:* stores goods till demand arrives — creates *time utility*.
  - *Inventory control:* decides how much stock to hold — too little breaks
    sales, too much freezes money.
  - *Order processing:* the paperwork-speed of receiving and filling orders —
    slow orders kill service reputations.

== Channels of distribution — the middlemen ladder

- *Meaning:* the channel is the *set of firms and individuals* that take the
  product from producer to consumer.
- *Zero-level channel (direct):* producer sells straight to the consumer —
  company outlets, door-to-door, online.
- *One-level channel:* producer $arrow$ *retailer* $arrow$ consumer — big
  retailers buying direct.
- *Two-level channel:* producer $arrow$ *wholesaler* $arrow$ *retailer* $arrow$
  consumer — the classic fast-moving consumer goods route.
- *Three-level channel:* producer $arrow$ *agent* $arrow$ wholesaler $arrow$
  retailer $arrow$ consumer — when the producer wants help reaching scattered
  markets.

== Factors determining the choice of channel

- *Product factors:* perishability demands short channels; high unit value
  and technical complexity favour direct selling.
- *Company factors:* funds available (own sales force vs hired middlemen),
  desire for control over the channel.
- *Market factors:* number of buyers, geographic spread, order size —
  scattered small buyers need long channels.
- *Competitive and legal factors:* follow or avoid rivals' channels; some
  products face legally prescribed routes.

