= Promotion — Telling, Persuading, Reminding

A great product at a fair price on every shelf still sells nothing if nobody
knows it exists. Promotion is the communication element — informing,
persuading and reminding buyers — and it deploys four tools together as the
*promotion mix*.

- *Advertising:* *paid, non-personal* communication through mass media —
  newspapers, TV, internet, hoardings; one message, millions reached.
- *Personal selling:* *face-to-face* persuasion by salespeople — flexible to
  each buyer, builds relationships, answers objections live.
- *Sales promotion:* *short-run incentives* — discounts, coupons, contests,
  free samples, festivals offers — designed to trigger a purchase now.
- *Public relations:* *unpaid image-building* — news stories, events,
  sponsorships, community work — credibility that advertising cannot buy.

== Advertising — role and objections

- *The role:* informs buyers about products and launches; persuades and
  reminds; widens markets; supports salespeople and dealers; builds brand
  image; and educates society (health, safety campaigns).
- *The objections:* adds to cost (and thus price); undermines social values
  by glorifying materialism; confuses buyers with exaggerated claims; and
  multiplies inferior products' appeal.
- *The standard replies:* large-scale advertising *lowers* per-unit cost by
  building volume; information empowers consumer choice; and regulation plus
  self-discipline punish false claims — the tool's misuse is no argument
  against the tool.

